This directory contains:

	README.rockmag	This document
	rmagcurie.c	rmagcurie C program code
	rmaghyst.c	rmaghyst C program code
	rockmag.c	Code shared between modules of this supplement
	rockmag.h	Declarations for rockmag.c
	test/		Example/test data and scripts

This package is intended to grow into a small toolkit for processing
and visualizing rock-magnetic data, complementing (not overlapping)
the "paleomag" supplement: paleomag handles directional (Dec/Inc)
demagnetization data, while rockmag handles field/temperature-versus-
moment data (hysteresis loops, IRM/backfield acquisition curves,
thermomagnetic M-T or kappa-T curves).

	rmagcurie	Estimate Curie temperature from a thermomagnetic
			heating/cooling curve. Each input segment (one
			run) is automatically split into monotonic
			heating/cooling branches, and each branch is
			analyzed for up to three candidate Curie-point
			estimates: the temperature of peak signal, the
			Curie-Weiss (1/chi vs T) intercept of Petrovsky
			& Kapicka (2006) - recommended for susceptibility
			data - and, for comparison, the classic two-tangent
			method of Gromme et al. (1969).

	rmaghyst	Hysteresis loop parameters. Fits the two high-field
			wings separately to separate the para-/diamagnetic
			slope chi from the saturation magnetization Ms, then
			reports Ms, the remanence Mrs, the coercivity Hc and
			Mrs/Ms, together with both wing fits so the quality
			of the correction is visible. Warns when Mrs/Ms > 1,
			which is physically impossible and means the loop
			never saturated within the measured field range.

Planned additions (not yet implemented):

	- IRM/backfield acquisition curves: coercivity of remanence Hcr,
	  coercivity spectra, and cumulative log-Gaussian (Robertson &
	  France, 1994; Kruiver et al., 2001) component unmixing.
	- The Day (1977)/Dunlop (2002) plot, which needs Mrs/Ms from
	  rmaghyst and Hcr from a backfield curve. Note this requires
	  loops that actually saturate: a VSM limited to ~900 Oe does not
	  saturate many rock samples, and then Ms - and any Day plot built
	  on it - is not recoverable from the measurement.

Input to rmagcurie is a simple 2-column table (temperature, magnetic
parameter), one thermomagnetic run per segment (">" header). See
test/rmagcurie_example.sh for a worked example (synthetic, exact-
recovery sanity check for both the Curie-Weiss and two-tangent
methods), and:

Petrovsky, E., and A. Kapicka (2006), On determination of the Curie
point from thermomagnetic curves, J. Geophys. Res., 111, B12S27,
doi:10.1029/2006JB004507.
